WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.720
A mysterious sex box, a 150 year long dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.920
and so many time loops. These sci-fi movies&nbsp;
still have audiences scratching their heads.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.720
Stanley Kubrick's "2001: A Space&nbsp;
Odyssey" is considered one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.160
great sci-fi classics — and one&nbsp;
of the most revered works in film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:22.560
history. The 1968 picture pushed&nbsp;
the boundaries of special effects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:28.800
lifted sci-fi from B-movie shlock to serious art,&nbsp;
and made everyone afraid of killer computers.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.080
After escaping the clutches of Hal 9000,&nbsp;
astronaut Dave Bowman is sucked into a trippy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.200
tunnel right outside Jupiter. He's assaulted&nbsp;
with all sorts of lights and crazy colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:45.040
before winding up in a cold, soulless hotel room.&nbsp;
In just a matter of minutes, Bowman turns into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:50.160
an old man — and that's when a black monolith&nbsp;
appears at the foot of his bed. These monoliths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.440
have been turning up the entire movie, sparking&nbsp;
new stages of evolution whenever they appear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.400
When Dave reaches out to the big black&nbsp;
obelisk, he turns into a glowing space fetus.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.360
This magical floating baby is known as the&nbsp;
Star Child, and in the film's final sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:10.080
it floats out into the galaxy, looking down&nbsp;
at Earth. Did Dave literally transform into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.120
an alien infant? Is the Star Child symbolic&nbsp;
of humanity's next stage in evolution?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:19.760
Could it mean something else altogether? It&nbsp;
doesn't seem like we'll ever know for sure.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.960
"Its origin — and purpose&nbsp;
— still a total mystery."

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.320
"A.I. Artificial Intelligence" is a thoroughly&nbsp;
depressing affair. After all, this is the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.080
where Haley Joel Osment breaks down crying because&nbsp;
his "mom" abandoned him in the woods. Granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:41.280
Osment is playing a lifelike android named David,&nbsp;
but still, you can't just toss a heartbroken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.960
"mecha" into the wild. After all, the world is&nbsp;
cruel to little androids. Poor David is bullied by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:52.000
human kids, sees his fellow robots ripped apart,&nbsp;
and eventually finds himself stuck at the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
of the ocean, desperately wishing he could become&nbsp;
a real boy. It's a wish that will never come true.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.200
And then there's that ending.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.200
After two thousand years of solitude, David is&nbsp;
rescued by evolved A.I. The creatures take pity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:10.320
on the little mecha, and using advanced tech,&nbsp;
they give David one last day with his mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.080
That's how the movie ends: with David cuddling&nbsp;
his mom, happy at last. Some casual viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.040
had no problem with the ending, but many sci-fi&nbsp;
fans were outraged at the shift in tone, placing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.560
the blame squarely on director Steven Spielberg.&nbsp;
"A.I." was originally a Stanley Kubrick project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.760
so many assumed Spielberg screwed up Kubrick's&nbsp;
dark project by tacking on the sappy finale.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.720
As Spielberg eventually pointed out, however, the&nbsp;
whole "David gets to spend one last day with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.600
mother" thing was actually Kubrick's idea.&nbsp;
More importantly, it isn't a happy ending:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.240
the evolved mechas are granting David's wish&nbsp;
because they're about to shut him down forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.080
They're going to kill this little kid, and&nbsp;
we're watching his final hallucination before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.840
he's put down. If that's not a Kubrick&nbsp;
ending, then we don't know what is.

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.080
Richard Kelly's cult classic "Donnie Darko" hit&nbsp;
theaters in 2001, but fans are still trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.440
solve this incredibly complex sci-fi flick to&nbsp;
this day. The plot follows the titular Donnie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:10.880
an angsty high schooler who narrowly avoids death&nbsp;
by a falling jet engine thanks to a six-foot-tall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:15.760
rabbit named Frank. If that isn't crazy enough,&nbsp;
the film soon introduces the concepts of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.800
travel and alternate dimensions, turning a&nbsp;
coming-of-age tale into a cryptic head-scratcher.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:21.440
"You're weird."

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:24.180
"Sorry."

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:26.240
"No, that was a compliment."

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.520
There's a lot of questions raised by "Donnie&nbsp;
Darko" that you probably won't see the answers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.320
on your first viewing. We know Donnie has to stop&nbsp;
an apocalypse, but what exactly does that have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:41.520
do with wormholes? How do deceased characters come&nbsp;
back to life, what's up with that demonic bunny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.440
and why is Donnie wearing that stupid&nbsp;
human suit? It's all so complicated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.440
Kelly felt the need to release a Director's&nbsp;
Cut to help fans put the pieces together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.440
"Donnie Darko" is a movie that demands&nbsp;
rewatch after rewatch, and while it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.160
possible to gain a decent understanding of the&nbsp;
plot, there's no denying that it's a mad movie.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:02.960
Before introducing the world to&nbsp;
the Cheddar Goblin in his 2018 film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.560
"Mandy," director Panos Cosmatos broke onto the&nbsp;
cinematic scene with "Beyond the Black Rainbow."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.200
This psychedelic nightmare of a movie features&nbsp;
a telekinetic girl and a slasher scientist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.840
who find themselves in a battle of life and&nbsp;
death. It's been compared to films like "El Topo"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.120
and "Eraserhead" — and according to its creator,&nbsp;
the film's screenplay is only 11 pages long.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.680
As you might expect, this is a movie that&nbsp;
takes its sweet time, drowning audiences with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.640
surreal images and sounds that make you wonder if&nbsp;
you're watching a film or going on an acid trip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:37.200
The movie prompts so many questions: what's&nbsp;
really happening at the Arboria Institute?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.840
What's up with that glowing crystal? And what&nbsp;
exactly happens to the villainous Dr. Nyle after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.800
he goes beyond the black rainbow? By the time you&nbsp;
come down from this incredibly disturbing trip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.480
you'll either immediately want more, or&nbsp;
you'll have completely lost your mind.

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.760
Ex Machina is a creepy film that examines what&nbsp;
might happen when humanity finally creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.720
artificial intelligence. And what might happen?&nbsp;
Nothing good. That's a lesson that Caleb learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.520
the hard way after he's invited to participate&nbsp;
in an advanced Turing test by billionaire tech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.240
genius Nathan. The programming prodigy wants&nbsp;
Caleb to see if his imprisoned android,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.440
an A.I. named Ava, is capable&nbsp;
of independent thought.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.680
The only hitch is that Ava looks like Alicia&nbsp;
Vikander, and Caleb starts to fall for her.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.160
"You're impressed."

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:23.040
"Haha! Yes, yes. Heh. Although—"

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.120
"'Although?' He he he. There's a&nbsp;
qualification to you being impressed?"

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.520
Ava and Caleb seem to grow close, and after&nbsp;
realizing Nathan is a monster who's creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.560
female androids for his own sick gratification,&nbsp;
Caleb decides to break Ava loose. He hopes they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.200
can run away together, and Ava seems totally&nbsp;
cool with Caleb's plan… but once she's free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.280
she kills Nathan and locks up Caleb,&nbsp;
leaving him to die as she sets out into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:50.080
the real world. Her decision left a lot of&nbsp;
viewers baffled: why kill such a nice guy?

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.440
Well, Caleb might not have been&nbsp;
as glaringly evil as Nathan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.120
but he only treats Ava like a human&nbsp;
once he starts falling in love with her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:02.000
Even though he's more polite, he still views Ava&nbsp;
like an object in the same way that Nathan does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.640
He only decides to help her escape after Ava&nbsp;
flirts with him and makes him feel special.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.880
Meanwhile, she's tired of being a slave, so&nbsp;
it's time for her to finally take control.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.320
Directed by Christopher Nolan, "Inception" is&nbsp;
a bizarre blockbuster where cities fold on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.120
of themselves, trains appear out of nowhere,&nbsp;
and hotels spin around like crashing cars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:25.200
In other words, it's a movie about the power&nbsp;
of dreams. "Inception" takes place in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.440
subconscious, where rules can be bent and&nbsp;
mental barriers manipulated — but for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:34.240
its incredible imagery, the film sparked so much&nbsp;
conversation because of a simple spinning top.

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:38.880
The plot of this sci-fi epic follows a group&nbsp;
of thieves, led by the enigmatic Dominick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.800
Cobb, as they try to implant an idea&nbsp;
in the mind of a young business heir.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:47.280
If Cobb can complete the mission, he'll have&nbsp;
his murky past erased, and he'll finally get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.000
to return to the U.S. and see his kids again. And&nbsp;
wouldn't you know it? The mission is a success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.400
and in the film's final moments, Cobb&nbsp;
is reunited with his children... maybe.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.560
There's actually a good chance that Cobb might&nbsp;
be asleep — stuck in limbo for the rest of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:05.360
life. Everything depends on that infamous top:&nbsp;
if Cobb is in the real world, it will topple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.920
over eventually — but if he's still asleep,&nbsp;
it will keep on spinning forever. The movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.640
cuts right as the top starts to wobble, which&nbsp;
resulted in endless debate about Cobb's fate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:19.280
According to Christopher Nolan, however, it's&nbsp;
not such a big deal if the top falls over or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:24.000
As the director explained, the important thing for&nbsp;
Cobb is that he's back with his kids. Whether this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.920
is reality or it's all in his head, the only&nbsp;
thing that matters is that it's real for him.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.680
Finding love is hard enough, but if you've only&nbsp;
got 45 days to find a mate or be morphed into an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.800
animal, that makes things a lot more difficult.&nbsp;
Unfortunately for poor David, if he doesn't find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.760
a partner in a few weeks, he'll be tossed into&nbsp;
a mysterious machine and turned into a lobster.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.480
However, things start looking up when he&nbsp;
meets the Shortsighted Woman. In this society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.200
partners have to share a physical similarity,&nbsp;
and since they both suffer from vision problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.960
it seems they're a match... until she is blinded.&nbsp;
This leaves the lovers in a tricky situation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.080
if they want to make this work,&nbsp;
David will have to blind himself too.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:03.920
"I'm going to do it with a knife."

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:06.480
"Do you want me to come with you?"

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:08.560
"I'd rather you didn't."

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:13.360
As the Shortsighted Woman waits at a diner booth,&nbsp;
David stands in front of the bathroom mirror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:18.000
with a steak knife just centimeters from his&nbsp;
own eyeball. Before we can see anything, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.160
the film cuts to the Shortsighted Woman&nbsp;
sitting at her table. She waits and waits for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:27.360
David to return, but the film goes black before&nbsp;
audiences can learn if David did the bloody deed.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.920
Not even the cast and crew of "The Lobster" know&nbsp;
the truth. Speaking with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:35.200
Colin Farrell revealed that director Yorgos&nbsp;
Lanthimos [YOR-gohs LAN-thih-mohs] and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.720
screenwriter Efthymis Filippou [EF-thee-mis&nbsp;
fih-LEE-poh] never came up with an answer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:43.600
the film's ending. As for himself, Farrell&nbsp;
can see it going either way. He joked:

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.080
"Honest to God, part of me thinks he does it.&nbsp;
Then part of me thinks that when the camera cuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:53.280
back to Rachel Weisz, I'm already in a f---ing&nbsp;
Uber, heading down the road as fast as I can."

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.360
The actor also raised the possibility&nbsp;
that David could have faked blindness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:00.880
so he could be with his lover while still&nbsp;
maintaining his eyesight. Whatever your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.040
interpretation might be, it probably says&nbsp;
a lot about the way you view romance.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.120
Written, directed by, and starring&nbsp;
Justin Benson and Aaron Moorhead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:14.240
2017's "The Endless" follows two brothers who&nbsp;
escaped a UFO cult when they were younger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.040
but are lured back by a strange video tape.&nbsp;
Once they arrive, freaky things start happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.720
and they start to get the feeling that&nbsp;
there's something evil hiding in the darkness.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:27.200
By the time the credits roll, "The Endless"&nbsp;
has posed a lot of complicated questions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.440
What's up with the entity and the time&nbsp;
loops? What's with those freaky statues?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.800
And why is there a crossover with&nbsp;
Benson and Moorhead's earlier movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:39.280
"Resolution?" Well, the invisible demon&nbsp;
haunting our heroes is a stand-in for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.920
horror movie audiences who like their stories&nbsp;
to have dark endings. And as it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:48.400
the creature drew the brothers back to the cult&nbsp;
in hopes of trapping them in a time loop. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:53.120
particular desert is crowded with different loops,&nbsp;
some which last seconds and others which stretch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:58.480
on for decades. If you die in the loop or submit&nbsp;
yourself to the beast, you'll be trapped forever.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.640
That's why the cult members look so young:&nbsp;
they've been repeating the same cycle every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.240
ten years. At the end of each loop, the entity&nbsp;
tears them to shreds, and they start over again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.240
It's all part of the creature's need&nbsp;
for scary stories with violent endings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:16.480
It turns out that the guys from "Resolution"&nbsp;
are stuck in a time loop of their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:19.520
a revelation that adds new depth to the 2012 film.

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:23.920
As for those monuments? They all represent&nbsp;
the evil entity. They were constructed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:28.720
the victims stuck in the loops, and they're meant&nbsp;
to express how these poor souls viewed the demon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.200
Judging by the apparent age&nbsp;
of some of these sculptures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.520
it seems like this inter-dimensional creature&nbsp;
has been trapping people for a very long time.

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:41.280
Christopher Nolan's 2020 sci-fi flick "Tenet"&nbsp;
just might be the filmmaker's most confusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:46.000
movie yet. Met with lukewarm reception by&nbsp;
critics, "Tenet" is a high-concept puzzle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.400
box that's more interested in giving the&nbsp;
viewer clues to solve it rather than answers.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:51.920
"The hell happened here?"

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.120
"It hasn't happened yet."

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.760
The Protagonist is a CIA agent, who finds a&nbsp;
mysterious artifact during a mission. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:08.560
the artifact is stolen from him, he's recruited&nbsp;
by a time-traveling organization called Tenet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.760
to retrieve it. The Protagonist and his handler&nbsp;
Neil travel the globe to recover the artifact, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:18.400
nothing — and no one — is as it seems. That sounds&nbsp;
straightforward enough, but things are never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.120
so simple in a Christopher Nolan movie, and the&nbsp;
ending of "Tenet" is one of his most confounding.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:26.880
The film's core narrative centers&nbsp;
around the concept of "time inversion."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:30.240
That means the entropy of objects&nbsp;
from the future can move backwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.640
so things from the future can get brought into&nbsp;
the past. Those in the present move forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:38.400
while the future moves backwards — at&nbsp;
least, in the present. To achieve this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:42.480
effect of two opposite movements at the&nbsp;
same time, Nolan shot the time inversion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:46.960
action sequences twice — one normally, and&nbsp;
the other with the actors moving backwards.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:50.880
Getting your head around the concept of time&nbsp;
inversion is the first key to unlocking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.480
rest of "Tenet." Most viewers are bound&nbsp;
to get lost their first time around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.240
as the movie breaks down linear time&nbsp;
to render it a circle. That's why even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:02.320
the movie's biggest defenders say it takes&nbsp;
another trip around to really get it to click.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:07.680
Whether it's the sight of a skeletal bear or a&nbsp;
man turned into a fungus, "Annihilation" is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:12.160
movie that sticks in your mind — but even more&nbsp;
compelling than the gigantic sharkagator is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:16.960
film's ambiguous ending. After heading deep into&nbsp;
the Shimmer — an ever-growing electromagnetic wave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:22.160
that mixes and matches DNA — biology professor&nbsp;
Lena encounters a metallic humanoid that tries to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:27.520
copy her likeness. Using a grenade, Lena kills&nbsp;
the doppelgänger and burns down the Shimmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:31.760
but while she's saved the world, that doesn't&nbsp;
mean it's smooth sailing for our hero.

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:36.080
Lena's whole reason for heading into the Shimmer&nbsp;
was to discover what happened to her husband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:41.120
Kane. He's a soldier who went MIA after heading&nbsp;
into the world's freakiest light show — and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:46.480
a year later, he suddenly appeared out of nowhere,&nbsp;
deathly ill. Hoping to save his life and ease her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:51.600
guilt, Lena accompanies four other women into the&nbsp;
Shimmer to figure out exactly what's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:56.320
What she learns is haunting: the Kane back at&nbsp;
the hospital is almost certainly a doppelgänger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.800
much like the one that tried to take Lena's place.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.360
However, there's something odd about the Lena&nbsp;
who finally escapes the Shimmer. When she hugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:07.600
her clone husband, we see the color in her&nbsp;
eyes dance and wave, much like the lights in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:12.400
the Shimmer. Did the real Lena make it back home,&nbsp;
or was she replaced by the doppelgänger somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:17.200
during their struggle? Or perhaps the Shimmer&nbsp;
altered her DNA and turned her into something new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:22.160
Whatever happened, Lena is a new person, forever&nbsp;
changed after literally confronting herself.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:27.280
Inspired by the French short film "La&nbsp;
Jetée," Terry Gilliam brings a fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:32.320
set of eyes to an old fashioned time travel&nbsp;
tale in "12 Monkeys." The killer cast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:37.600
gorgeous production design are enough to get&nbsp;
viewers to still return to this 1995 classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:39.920
even if the ending is a bit mind-boggling.

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:45.600
In a post-apocalyptic 2035, James Cole is a&nbsp;
prison inmate sent back in time to stop the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:50.880
virus that destroyed humanity. He travels back&nbsp;
to 1996 to track down the source of the virus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:56.160
but is continuously haunted by dreams of a little&nbsp;
boy seeing a man shot at an airport. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.800
he finally catches up with Dr. Peters, who's&nbsp;
heading to an airport to start the virus outbreak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:05.840
Cole tries to stop the doctor and instead is&nbsp;
shot by the police. As it turns out, his dreams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:10.320
weren't dreams at all. They were memories of his&nbsp;
younger self watching his future self get killed.

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:15.360
The purpose of Cole's tragic life is just one of&nbsp;
many questions left at the end of "12 Monkeys."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:19.920
We never get to see if any of Cole's actions&nbsp;
change the timeline, but it's heavily implied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:24.880
that his actions were fruitless. Nothing will&nbsp;
change. The past stays the same and the future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:30.320
is unavoidable. The whole affair is a bleak take&nbsp;
on time travel that appreciates repeat viewings.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:34.080
"The movie never changes, it… can't change, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:36.800
every time you see it, it seems&nbsp;
different because you were different."

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:40.880
"Stalker" is a classic, but by today's&nbsp;
standards, it's definitely a slow movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:46.800
Watching Andrei Tarkovsky's 1979 cerebral&nbsp;
work of post-apocalyptic sci-fi is a test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:51.360
of patience — but for those who have stuck with&nbsp;
it, the reward has been great. Over the years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:54.480
many people have watched this movie&nbsp;
and walked away with different stabs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:59.440
at its meaning. A deeply interpretive film,&nbsp;
"Stalker" is as confounding as it is profound.

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:02.480
Based on Arkady and Boris Strugatsky's novella&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.680
"Roadside Picnic," three men&nbsp;
— the writer, the professor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:10.880
and the Stalker — go into the radioactive "Zone,"&nbsp;
which contains a mythical wish-granting room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:15.760
The trio ventures through a dangerous land of&nbsp;
paranormal anomalies and unexplained phenomena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:20.240
all while philosophically sparring with each other&nbsp;
and exploring their motivations for the journey.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:24.880
When they finally reach the Room, however,&nbsp;
there is an inexplicable anti-climax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:29.600
as the three men sit in silence. What happens&nbsp;
inside the room and the significance of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:34.560
journey is left entirely ambiguous. What&nbsp;
exactly is the Zone, and how has it changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:38.800
the people who dare enter it? The final shots&nbsp;
of the movie show that the Stalker's daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:43.120
has now gained superhuman powers, which&nbsp;
are implied to be a result of his journey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:46.800
This question and others are asked&nbsp;
from the very beginning of "Stalker,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.800 --> 00:15:50.960
and its lack of answers still sparks&nbsp;
vigorous discussion over 40 years later.

00:15:58.242 --> 00:16:04.002
"Predestination" is based off of Robert A.&nbsp;
Heinlein's 1959 science fiction short story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.002 --> 00:16:10.402
"All You Zombies." This 2014 sci-fi puzzle stars&nbsp;
Hawke as Agent Doe, a disfigured time-traveling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.402 --> 00:16:15.282
agent who moonlights as a barkeep after a failed&nbsp;
attempt to stop the bomber that wounded him.

00:16:15.282 --> 00:16:20.002
One day in 1970, Agent Doe meets a man&nbsp;
called John, who reveals that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.002 --> 00:16:25.522
born an intersex female named Jane and had&nbsp;
a child, who was stolen by an unknown man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.522 --> 00:16:30.482
Complications during birth led to forced gender&nbsp;
reassignment surgery, so Jane began living as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.482 --> 00:16:36.802
John. Agent Doe takes John back in time to 1963 to&nbsp;
get revenge on the man who created this situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.802 --> 00:16:41.282
only to realize that John is the person&nbsp;
who got Jane — himself — pregnant. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.282 --> 00:16:46.962
Agent Doe takes Jane's baby further back in&nbsp;
time to 1945, and the child grows up into Jane.

00:16:46.962 --> 00:16:51.922
This is the predestination paradox, and it only&nbsp;
gets more complicated as the movie continues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.922 --> 00:16:55.122
By the end, it is revealed the&nbsp;
movie is about a single character:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.122 --> 00:16:59.282
the agent and the bomber are also the&nbsp;
same person as John, Jane, and the baby.

00:16:59.922 --> 00:17:04.082
"It's a paradox, right, but it&nbsp;
can't be para-doctored, heheheheh."

00:17:04.082 --> 00:17:08.802
After learning who he will become, the agent&nbsp;
kills the bomber and tries to close his own loop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.802 --> 00:17:12.402
That's where "Predestination" ends,&nbsp;
leaving us to imagine John's endless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.402 --> 00:17:16.482
nightmare and wonder about the logic&nbsp;
and existential questions behind it all.

00:17:17.042 --> 00:17:21.762
Robert Pattinson fans might follow the dreamy&nbsp;
actor to the ends of the Earth, but apparently not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.762 --> 00:17:27.122
beyond our solar system. At least, that's how the&nbsp;
reaction to 2018's "High Life" would make it seem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.122 --> 00:17:32.242
While many critics found Claire Denis' erotic&nbsp;
sci-fi drama mesmerizing and thought-provoking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.242 --> 00:17:35.762
a majority of the audience seemed&nbsp;
to disagree. This is likely due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.762 --> 00:17:39.922
the slow pacing and cryptic methods&nbsp;
of storytelling that Denis utilizes.

00:17:39.922 --> 00:17:44.722
Monte is a criminal on death row, who's been&nbsp;
placed on a ship full of convicted killers heading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.722 --> 00:17:49.202
towards a black hole. He and the others are&nbsp;
subject to experiments run by the mysterious Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.202 --> 00:17:54.562
Dibs, who controls sexual activity on the ship.&nbsp;
Monte manages to father a child, and he works to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.562 --> 00:17:59.922
keep himself and his baby alive amidst the intense&nbsp;
psychological pressure of Dr. Dibs' experiments.

00:17:59.922 --> 00:18:03.362
"High Life" is focused on sex, but&nbsp;
many viewers are still unsure about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.362 --> 00:18:07.682
what exactly the movie has to say about&nbsp;
sexuality thanks to its dream-like quality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.682 --> 00:18:12.482
Its most puzzling aspect is its representation&nbsp;
of "The Box," a room that the prisoners use to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.482 --> 00:18:17.442
stimulate themselves. Denis described herself&nbsp;
as "pro-sex" in an interview with Slate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.442 --> 00:18:22.722
clarified that she sees The Box as something&nbsp;
positive. However, in an interview with GQ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.722 --> 00:18:27.042
she also said that she herself doesn't actually&nbsp;
know what "High Life" is about, saying —

00:18:27.042 --> 00:18:30.402
"I'm not interested in understanding,&nbsp;
honestly. It's not a joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.402 --> 00:18:34.082
I don't need to understand, I need to feel&nbsp;
good about something, that's all I need."

00:18:34.722 --> 00:18:40.162
Although Cameron Crowe's 2001 sci-fi adventure&nbsp;
was a critical punchline in its day, "Vanilla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.162 --> 00:18:44.802
Sky" has gone on to gain a cult following.&nbsp;
This ambitious thriller is an English-language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.802 --> 00:18:50.562
remake of Alejandro Amenábar's "Open Your Eyes," a&nbsp;
trippy warning about the dangers of technology and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.562 --> 00:18:56.162
vanity. Narcissistic playboy David Aames is in a&nbsp;
horrific car accident after his jealous girlfriend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.162 --> 00:19:01.122
Julie discovers his relationship with another&nbsp;
woman, Sofia. After recovering, David rejoins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.122 --> 00:19:06.562
society and starts dating Sofia again, but his&nbsp;
reality starts to disintegrate in strange ways.

00:19:06.562 --> 00:19:10.802
The ending of "Vanilla Sky" reveals that the&nbsp;
movie has taken place inside David's lucid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.802 --> 00:19:17.202
dream that has been going on for 150 years. After&nbsp;
disfiguring his face and losing Sofia in reality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.202 --> 00:19:22.402
David opted to exist in a cryogenic hyper-sleep&nbsp;
inside a dream, where he can live a fantasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.402 --> 00:19:27.682
life until the technology needed to repair his&nbsp;
face is perfected. His idealized fantasy world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.682 --> 00:19:31.362
gets ruined by a glitch in the system,&nbsp;
so David is given the choice to either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.362 --> 00:19:35.362
stay in the dream or leap off the roof to&nbsp;
break the illusion and return to reality.

00:19:36.242 --> 00:19:42.322
"Look at us. I'm frozen and&nbsp;
you're dead. And I love you."

00:19:43.042 --> 00:19:47.522
What David ultimately chooses and what it&nbsp;
represents is unclear. He decides to jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.522 --> 00:19:51.842
and break free from this dream, but he doesn't&nbsp;
know for certain if he will wake up — or what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.842 --> 00:19:56.962
world will even look like now after so much time.&nbsp;
In the final moments, we hear a woman's voice call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.962 --> 00:20:01.522
to him, but it's not clear whether he has made&nbsp;
it to reality or if he has finally passed away.

00:20:02.322 --> 00:20:07.282
The bittersweet romantic drama "Eternal Sunshine&nbsp;
of the Spotless Mind" is a collaboration between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.282 --> 00:20:12.402
surrealism experts Charlie Kaufman and Michel&nbsp;
Gondry. When Joel and Clementine break up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.402 --> 00:20:16.482
they both decide to undergo a procedure to&nbsp;
have their memories of each other erased.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.482 --> 00:20:20.962
The majority of the film takes place in Joel's&nbsp;
mind, as he recalls his doomed relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.962 --> 00:20:25.282
Clementine as the memories get deleted. After&nbsp;
going through the procedure, he retains one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.282 --> 00:20:29.602
vague recollection of Clementine telling him&nbsp;
to meet her in Montauk, where they first met.

00:20:29.602 --> 00:20:34.562
Despite the procedure's success, both lovers&nbsp;
find each other again in Montauk and start again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.562 --> 00:20:38.002
even after learning the truth of their&nbsp;
chaotic and painful relationship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.002 --> 00:20:41.282
This might seem to defeat the point&nbsp;
of the sci-fi elements — or even the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.282 --> 00:20:44.882
story as a whole — but it speaks to&nbsp;
the movie's central theme of love.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.882 --> 00:20:48.322
These two have a special connection,&nbsp;
and nothing can get in the way of that.

00:20:48.322 --> 00:20:52.002
It's a hopeful ending, although seeing&nbsp;
the way Clementine and Joel's relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.002 --> 00:20:55.762
plays out makes one wonder if they're&nbsp;
doomed to repeat the same mistakes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.762 --> 00:20:59.122
Should these two really be together? Or&nbsp;
is the love that was strong enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.122 --> 00:21:03.442
bring them back together also enough to break&nbsp;
their old dynamic? According to Vanity Fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.442 --> 00:21:08.562
Kaufman originally conceived of a darker ending,&nbsp;
in which Joel walks away after learning the truth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.562 --> 00:21:12.882
Knowing that Gondry and Kaufman disagreed over&nbsp;
whether Joel and Clementine should end up together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.882 --> 00:21:22.242
further enhances the uncertainty of what&nbsp;
comes next for this eccentric couple.

